  "question": {
    "text": "A computer producing factory has only two plants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">T</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">T</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. Plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">T</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></math> produces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">T</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub></math> produces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the total computers produced. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of computers produced in the factory turn out to be defective. It is known that,&nbsp;<br />\r<br />\r<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>computer</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>turns</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>out</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>to</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>be</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>defective</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>given</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>that</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>it</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>is</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>produced</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>in</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>plant</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>computer</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>turns</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>out</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>to</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>be</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>defective</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>given</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>that</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>it</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>is</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>produced</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>in</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>plant</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></math>.<br />\r<br />\rWhere,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mo>)</mo></math> denotes the probability of an event <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></math>. A computer produced in the factory is randomly selected and it does not turn out to be defective. Then the probability that it is produced in plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"italic\">T</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is",
